<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<rdf:RDF
  xmlns:mailo="https://w3id.org/mailo#"
  xmlns:rdfs="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#"
  xmlns:owl="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#"
  xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#"
  xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/"
  xmlns:eli="http://data.europa.eu/eli/ontology#"
  xmlns:skos="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#"
>
  <owl:Ontology rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#">
    <dcterms:created rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-04-30</dcterms:created>
    <dcterms:creator>Victor Kao</dcterms:creator>
    <dcterms:description>OWL ontology mapping EU regulatory frameworks (GDPR, AI Act, MDR, PLD, EHDS, DGA, Data Act) to medical AI automated decision-making, CJEU case law, UPC patent litigation, and compliance obligations.</dcterms:description>
    <dcterms:license rdf:resource="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"/>
    <dcterms:modified rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-09-10</dcterms:modified>
    <dcterms:publisher>KU Leuven, Faculty of Engineering Science</dcterms:publisher>
    <dcterms:title>Medical AI Legal Ontology (MAILO)</dcterms:title>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Version 5.0.2 (patch): corrected the ECLI recorded for C-203/22 (Dun &amp; Bradstreet Austria) from the erroneous ECLI:EU:C:2025:145 to the correct ECLI:EU:C:2025:117.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Version 5.1.0: corrective release consolidating four rounds of work, none previously deployed. (1) All nine CJEU paragraph citations were re-checked and all nine corrected; two substantive mischaracterisations were removed (the health-status exclusion is categorical under PNR Directive Art.6(4), not curable by a GDPR Art.9(2) basis; the strict-necessity test does not entail invalidity, the Court having upheld the Directive by Charter-conforming interpretation). (2) The patent, EPO and UPC instances were verified: T 1191/19 re-dated and its holding rewritten, T 0161/18 corrected to a refused application, the citesPrecedent graph repaired, litigation statuses refreshed. (3) Legislative citations were audited: all four EHDS articles carried proposal numbering and were renumbered, Art.73 was added, PLD Art.11 was corrected from the causation presumption to exemption from liability, DGA Art.31 was corrected to Art.29, and AI Act Art.50 was relabelled. (4) MAILO's boundaries were made explicit: every case now declares an InclusionBasis and every principle an EncodingBasis, separating what a court held from what the ontology designer inferred or operationalised. Correcting the hierarchy that let the boundary errors pass, AutomatedDecisionSystem is no longer a subclass of MedicalAISystem — the entailment ran backwards, making every automated decision system medical. Parts IV and V of the SHACL shapes now enforce citation integrity and boundary declaration on the graph itself, and each shape has been tested against the error it was written for.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Version 5.2.0 — second corrective release, 10 September 2026. Continues the audit begun in v5.1.0, which covered the CJEU paragraph citations, the patent and EPO instances, an initial set of legislative citations, and the inclusion and encoding boundaries. This release audits everything that remained: the cross-framework tensions, the principles and cases outside the trilogy, the GDPR, AI Act, MDR, Data Act and Trade Secrets Directive provisions, and the SHACL shapes. Coverage rises from 48 to 71 articles.

The recurring defect is not miscitation but misattribution of normative character. Two provisions had their effect inverted and two pairs were transposed.

CROSS-FRAMEWORK TENSIONS. None of the ten was sound. Four cited the wrong provision: DGA neutrality is Art.12(1)(a) not Art.10; Data Act trade-secret safeguards are Arts.4(6)-(8) and 5(9)-(11) not Art.15; the EHDS opt-out tension still carried proposal numbering; and the PLD tension attributed the defect presumption to Art.11 rather than Art.10(4). Two cited C-203/22 at paras 60-70 instead of 59-62. Seven instances used conflictType with hasResolutionStatus while three used conflictsWith with involvesFramework, so any cross-tension query silently omitted a third of the set; the model is harmonised. Tension_BlackBox_PatentProtection carries a warning that it conflates patent protection with secrecy — a patent requires disclosure, as T 161/18 and T 1191/19 both demonstrate.

GDPR. Art.22(1) had been recorded as a flat prohibition, pre-committing to one side of a contested question C-634/21 did not settle, and omitting profiling. Art.22(3) had been described as applying whenever a derogation applies; it attaches only to points (a) and (c) of Art.22(2), which matters for medical AI running on a national legal basis. Art.22(4) had been called 'enhanced protection' for special category data when it is a prohibition admitting only Art.9(2)(a) or (g) — consequential because health data sits squarely in Art.9(1). Art.15(1)(h) had omitted the existence limb and the cross-reference scoping it to Art.22(1) and (4).

AI ACT. Art.86's right to explanation is confined to Annex III systems. Medical devices reach high-risk status by the Annex I route under Art.6(1), so Art.86 does not apply to a diagnostic or imaging AI device, and neither does the Art.26(11) duty to inform affected persons; for that population GDPR Arts.15(1)(h) and 22 carry the explanation duty alone. A HighRiskRoute vocabulary encodes the distinction, and MDR Annex VIII Rule 11 — the classification rule that produces it, previously absent — is linked through triggersRoute. Art.13 addresses DEPLOYERS, not affected persons, which is the pivot of the trade-secret argument. Art.10(3) had dropped the qualifier 'to the best extent possible'.

OTHER INSTRUMENTS. TSD Art.5 had been read as disapplying secrecy where information rights are exercised; Art.5(a) is the freedom of expression of Charter Art.11, and the provision that preserves regulatory disclosure duties is Art.1(2)(b), now added along with Art.3 on reverse engineering. PLD Arts.4 and 7 were transposed and Art.5, the strict-liability rule itself, was missing. Data Act Art.3 is a design duty and Art.4 the access right, with different application dates; Art.17 is the request procedure, not public-emergency sharing. Six EHDS provisions were added, of which Art.53(1)(e)(ii) matters most: it expressly permits secondary use for training, testing and evaluating algorithms in medical devices and AI systems, the legal pathway on which medical-AI training under the EHDS rests. EHDS Art.52, governing IP and trade secrets in secondary use, was also absent.

CASES AND SHAPES. G 1/19 is a COMVIK inventive-step ruling, and the Enlarged Board held that whether the simulated system is technical is NOT decisive; all three EPO instances were attributed to EPC Art.52 and are re-anchored to Arts.56, 83 and 84. C-307/22 (FT v DW) involves no AI. Four cases are rescoped, leaving two of sixteen with a verified medical-AI technology claim. Correcting the class hierarchy in v5.1.0 inverted what shape target classes mean, since targeting AutomatedDecisionSystem was narrower than MedicalAISystem before and is broader after; three C-817/19 shapes had drifted and all five now target MedicalAISystem. MedicalAIComplianceShape records that its C-817/19 constraints are transposed rather than held, and that its conjunctive structure is a modelling choice: conformance evidences alignment with MAILO's model, not compliance with law.

NOT VERIFIED. Three CJEU cases lack ECLIs, deliberately not supplied from memory. Three UPC instances retain unverified substantive assertions, flagged on the instances. No finding in either corrective release has been reviewed by a second EU data-protection specialist.</rdfs:comment>
    <owl:priorVersion rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo/5.1.0"/>
    <owl:versionIRI rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo/5.2.0"/>
    <owl:versionInfo>5.2.0</owl:versionInfo>
  </owl:Ontology>
  <owl:DatatypeProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#art22ExceptionApplied">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Art.22 exception applied</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment>contract_necessity | explicit_consent | member_state_law</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The GDPR Art.22(2) exception relied on: 'contract_necessity' | 'explicit_consent' | 'member_state_law'. Must be stated or Art.22(1) prohibition applies (C-634/21 P_634_004).</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#AutomatedDecisionSystem"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </owl:DatatypeProperty>
  <mailo:CJEUCase rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#C_307_22">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">C-307/22 FT v DW (Medical Records Access)</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2023-10-26</dcterms:date>
    <dcterms:identifier>C-307/22</dcterms:identifier>
    <dcterms:identifier>ECLI:EU:C:2023:811</dcterms:identifier>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Inclusion basis (DirectSubjectMatter): Access to medical records under GDPR Art.15 in a medical context.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">SCOPE CORRECTION (v5.1.0): this case involved NO artificial intelligence. It concerned dental records and the cost of a subject access request. The instance previously asserted involvesTechnology MedicalAISystem, an interpretesArticle link to GDPR Art.15(1)(h) (the logic of automated decision-making, not at issue here), and an outcome mentioning AI-generated diagnostic data. All three are removed. Note that this error survived the Part V technology constraint because the case was declared DirectSubjectMatter: that shape tests whether a medical AI claim is consistent with the declared basis, not whether the declared basis is itself correct.</rdfs:comment>
    <skos:notation>ECLI:EU:C:2023:811</skos:notation>
    <mailo:decidedBy>
      <mailo:Court rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#CJEU_FirstChamber">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">CJEU First Chamber</rdfs:label>
      </mailo:Court>
    </mailo:decidedBy>
    <mailo:establishesPrinciple>
      <mailo:RatioDecidendi rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#P_307_001">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">P-307/22-001: Free first copy of medical records under GDPR Art.15(3)</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A patient is entitled to a first copy of his or her medical records free of charge, without having to give reasons, and to a faithful and intelligible reproduction extending to full documents where that is necessary to understand the personal data they contain (Arts.12(5), 15(1) and 15(3) GDPR). CORRECTED in v5.1.0: the earlier text described an 'unconditional' right covering 'all AI-generated diagnostic outputs'. The judgment concerns neither AI nor an unconditional entitlement — Art.15(3) allows a reasonable fee for further copies, and Art.15(4) preserves the rights of others.</rdfs:comment>
        <mailo:appliesTo rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#AutomatedDecisionSystem"/>
        <mailo:hasEncodingBasis rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#CourtHolding"/>
        <mailo:hasPrecedenceLevel rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">binding_ratio</mailo:hasPrecedenceLevel>
        <mailo:triggersObligation rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#DisclosureObligation"/>
        <mailo:triggersObligation rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#TransparencyObligation"/>
      </mailo:RatioDecidendi>
    </mailo:establishesPrinciple>
    <mailo:hasInclusionBasis>
      <mailo:InclusionBasis rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#DomainContext">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Domain context</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Retained as background for a cross-framework tension rather than for any principle it establishes.</rdfs:comment>
      </mailo:InclusionBasis>
    </mailo:hasInclusionBasis>
    <mailo:hasOutcome>First Chamber, 26 October 2023 (FT v DW): a dentist refused a patient a free first copy of his medical records, relying on German law requiring patients to bear the cost. The Court held that Arts.12(5) and 15(1) and (3) GDPR oblige the controller to supply a first copy free of charge irrespective of the requester's reasons, that Art.23(1)(i) does not permit national law to shift that cost to protect the controller's economic interests, and that in a doctor-patient relationship the copy must be a faithful and intelligible reproduction, extending to full documents where necessary to understand the data.</mailo:hasOutcome>
    <mailo:hasStatus rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Final — binding precedent</mailo:hasStatus>
    <mailo:interpretesArticle>
      <mailo:LegalArticle rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#GDPR_Art12_5">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">GDPR Art.12(5) — Exercise of data subject rights free of charge</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Information and action under Arts.15-22 are provided free of charge; a reasonable fee or refusal is possible only for manifestly unfounded or excessive requests, and the controller bears the burden of showing this. Basis, with Art.15(3), for the first-copy holding in C-307/22. Added in v5.1.0.</rdfs:comment>
      </mailo:LegalArticle>
    </mailo:interpretesArticle>
    <mailo:interpretesArticle>
      <mailo:LegalArticle rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#GDPR_Art15">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">GDPR Article 15</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Right of access by the data subject.</rdfs:comment>
        <mailo:grantsRight rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#Right_MedicalRecordAccess"/>
      </mailo:LegalArticle>
    </mailo:interpretesArticle>
    <mailo:interpretesArticle>
      <mailo:LegalArticle rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#GDPR_Art15_3">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">GDPR Art.15(3) — Right to a copy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The controller shall provide a copy of the personal data undergoing processing; a reasonable fee based on administrative costs may be charged for further copies. Interpreted in C-307/22 as requiring a faithful and intelligible reproduction, extending to full documents where necessary to understand the data. Added in v5.1.0.</rdfs:comment>
      </mailo:LegalArticle>
    </mailo:interpretesArticle>
    <mailo:interpretesArticle>
      <mailo:LegalArticle rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#GDPR_Art23">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">GDPR Art.23 — Restrictions</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Union or Member State law may restrict the scope of Arts.12-22 where the restriction respects the essence of fundamental rights and is necessary and proportionate. Held in C-307/22 not to permit national law to shift the cost of a first copy onto the data subject in order to protect the controller's economic interests. Added in v5.1.0.</rdfs:comment>
      </mailo:LegalArticle>
    </mailo:interpretesArticle>
  </mailo:CJEUCase>
  <owl:DatatypeProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#usesHealthStatusAsCriterion">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">uses health status as criterion</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment>Must be false — health status excluded from automated criteria (C-817/19 P_817_004)</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">True if the system uses an individual's health status as an automated classification criterion. Must be false (C-817/19 P_817_004 Grand Chamber prohibition).</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#AutomatedDecisionSystem"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean"/>
  </owl:DatatypeProperty>
  <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#hasObligation">
    <rdfs:comment>Framework imposes an obligation</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#RegulatoryFramework"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#ComplianceObligation"/>
  </owl:ObjectProperty>
  <owl:DatatypeProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#proposedRenumberingTo">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">proposed renumbering to</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The article designation the provision would carry if the proposal is adopted as drafted. A string, not an IRI: the target provision does not exist in law and must not be resolvable as though it did.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#PendingAmendment"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </owl:DatatypeProperty>
  <mailo:LiteratureSource rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#lit_Pandit_H_J_et_al_2024_Data_Privacy_Vocabulary_DPV">
    <rdfs:label>Pandit, H. J. et al. (2024). Data Privacy Vocabulary (DPV) – Version 2.0. Springer / W3C DPVCG.</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:date>2024</dcterms:date>
    <dcterms:description>RDFS/SKOS + OWL2 alt; 911 concepts; serialised in TTL/JSON-LD/RDF-XML; namespace http://w3id.org/dpv#. Foundational vocabulary the thesis should reuse. Recommendation: import DPV namespace into MAILO; align mailo:hasObligation with dpv:hasLegalBasis.</dcterms:description>
    <dcterms:source rdf:resource="https://w3c.github.io/dpv/2.1/dpv/"/>
    <mailo:relevanceScore rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</mailo:relevanceScore>
  </mailo:LiteratureSource>
  <owl:DatatypeProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#identifiesInputFactors">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">identifies input factors</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment>Explanation identifies which inputs affected the output</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">True if the explanation identifies which specific input data affected the automated output and the main reasoning path (C-203/22 P_203_001; GDPR Art.15(1)(h)).</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#Explanation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean"/>
  </owl:DatatypeProperty>
  <owl:DatatypeProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#hasDefinedScope">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">has defined scope</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment>Tri-level proportionality (a): targeted scope (C-817/19 P_817_003)</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Tri-level proportionality test (a): targeted scope. C-817/19 P_817_003.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#AutomatedDecisionSystem"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean"/>
  </owl:DatatypeProperty>
  <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#involvesPatent">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">involves patent</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment>UPC case involves a patent</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#UPCCase"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#Patent"/>
  </owl:ObjectProperty>
  <mailo:CJEUCase rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#C_311_18">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">C-311/18 Schrems II</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2020-07-16</dcterms:date>
    <dcterms:identifier>C-311/18</dcterms:identifier>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Inclusion basis (DomainContext): Schrems II. Background for cross-border health data transfer under EHDS.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">MISSING METADATA (v5.1.0): no ECLI recorded. Not supplied here because it was not verified against a primary source in this pass, and asserting an identifier from memory is the error class that produced the original C-203/22 defect. Add after checking EUR-Lex.</rdfs:comment>
    <mailo:hasInclusionBasis rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#DomainContext"/>
    <mailo:hasStatus rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Final</mailo:hasStatus>
  </mailo:CJEUCase>
  <mailo:CJEUCase rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#C_203_22">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">C-203/22 Dun &amp; Bradstreet Austria</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2025-02-27</dcterms:date>
    <dcterms:identifier>C-203/22</dcterms:identifier>
    <dcterms:identifier>ECLI:EU:C:2025:117</dcterms:identifier>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Inclusion basis (TransposedPrinciple): Telecoms credit scoring. The Art.15(1)(h) explanation standard is transposed to medical AI explanations.</rdfs:comment>
    <skos:notation>ECLI:EU:C:2025:117</skos:notation>
    <mailo:citesPrecedent>
      <mailo:CJEUCase rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#C_634_21">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">C-634/21 SCHUFA Holding (Scoring)</rdfs:label>
        <dcterms:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2023-12-07</dcterms:date>
        <dcterms:identifier>C-634/21</dcterms:identifier>
        <dcterms:identifier>ECLI:EU:C:2023:957</dcterms:identifier>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Inclusion basis (TransposedPrinciple): Consumer credit scoring. The Art.22(1) analysis is transposed to clinical risk-scoring systems.</rdfs:comment>
        <skos:notation>ECLI:EU:C:2023:957</skos:notation>
        <mailo:citesPrecedent rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#C_817_19"/>
        <mailo:decidedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#CJEU_FirstChamber"/>
        <mailo:establishesPrinciple rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#P_634_001"/>
        <mailo:establishesPrinciple rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#P_634_002"/>
        <mailo:establishesPrinciple rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#P_634_003"/>
        <mailo:establishesPrinciple rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#P_634_004"/>
        <mailo:hasInclusionBasis rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#TransposedPrinciple"/>
        <mailo:hasOutcome rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">GDPR Art.22(1) constitutes a prohibition; automated scoring by credit agency = 'decision' triggering Art.22; 'solely' test is functional not formal.</mailo:hasOutcome>
        <mailo:hasStatus rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Final — binding precedent</mailo:hasStatus>
        <mailo:interpretesArticle rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#GDPR_Art15_1h"/>
        <mailo:interpretesArticle rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#GDPR_Art22"/>
        <mailo:interpretesArticle rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#GDPR_Art22_1"/>
        <mailo:interpretesArticle rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#GDPR_Art22_2"/>
        <mailo:involvesTechnology rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#RiskScoringSystem"/>
      </mailo:CJEUCase>
    </mailo:citesPrecedent>
    <mailo:decidedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#CJEU_FirstChamber"/>
    <mailo:establishesPrinciple>
      <mailo:RatioDecidendi rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#P_203_001">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">P-203/22-001: Meaningful Explainability Standard</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Art.15(1)(h) requires actionable explanations in non-technical language, identifying which inputs affected the output and the main reasoning path. Abstract mathematical formulas are insufficient.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Encoding basis (DesignerOperationalisation): Para 61 requires the data subject to understand which personal data were used and in what way. identifiesInputFactors is MAILO's predicate for that standard.</rdfs:comment>
        <mailo:appliesTo rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#AutomatedDecisionSystem"/>
        <mailo:appliesTo rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#MedicalAISystem"/>
        <mailo:hasEncodingBasis rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#DesignerOperationalisation"/>
        <mailo:hasPrecedenceLevel rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">binding_ratio</mailo:hasPrecedenceLevel>
        <mailo:triggersObligation rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#ExplainabilityObligation"/>
      </mailo:RatioDecidendi>
    </mailo:establishesPrinciple>
    <mailo:hasInclusionBasis>
      <mailo:InclusionBasis rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#TransposedPrinciple">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Transposed principle</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The case establishes a principle in another domain that MAILO applies to medical AI by structural analogy. The transposition is the ontology designer's step, not the court's, and the affected shapes say so.</rdfs:comment>
      </mailo:InclusionBasis>
    </mailo:hasInclusionBasis>
    <mailo:hasOutcome rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Actionable explanations required (not algorithm formulas); explanation must enable effective exercise of Art.22(3) remedies.</mailo:hasOutcome>
    <mailo:hasStatus rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Final — binding precedent</mailo:hasStatus>
    <mailo:interpretesArticle>
      <mailo:LegalArticle rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#GDPR_Art15_1h">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">GDPR Art.15(1)(h) — Automated decision-making: existence, logic, significance and consequences</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The data subject has the right to be informed of THE EXISTENCE of automated decision-making, including profiling, REFERRED TO IN ART.22(1) AND (4) and, at least in those cases, meaningful information about the logic involved as well as the significance and the envisaged consequences of such processing. Interpreted in C-203/22 paras 59-62. CORRECTED in v5.1.0: the earlier text omitted both the existence limb and the cross-reference scoping this right to Art.22(1) and (4) cases, which is what ties the explanation duty to the automated-decision regime rather than to processing at large.</rdfs:comment>
        <mailo:grantsRight rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#Right_Explanation"/>
      </mailo:LegalArticle>
    </mailo:interpretesArticle>
    <mailo:interpretesArticle>
      <mailo:LegalArticle rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#GDPR_Art22">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">GDPR Article 22</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Prohibition on solely automated individual decision-making.</rdfs:comment>
      </mailo:LegalArticle>
    </mailo:interpretesArticle>
  </mailo:CJEUCase>
  <owl:DatatypeProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#isHighRiskAIAct">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">is high-risk under AI Act</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment>True if system falls under AI Act Annex III high-risk categories</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">True if the system falls within AI Act Annex III high-risk categories (medical device AI, biometric, critical infrastructure).</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#MedicalAISystem"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean"/>
  </owl:DatatypeProperty>
  <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#requiresAssessment">
    <rdfs:comment>Technology requires a conformity path</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#Technology"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#ConformityAssessment"/>
