  <mailo:LiteratureSource rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#lit_Rebedea_T_et_al_2023_NeMo_Guardrails_A_Toolkit_for_Co">
    <rdfs:label>Rebedea, T. et al. (2023). NeMo Guardrails: A Toolkit for Controllable and Safe LLM Applications with Programmable Rails. EMNLP Demo. arXiv:2310.10501.</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:date>2023</dcterms:date>
    <dcterms:description>Colang DSL + dialogue-state engine; rules user-defined and interpretable. 2025 update: NIM micro-services with ~0.5s added latency for ~50% protection lift. Establishes programmable-rail paradigm; uses ad-hoc DSL not formal ontology.</dcterms:description>
    <dcterms:source rdf:resource="https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.10501"/>
    <mailo:relevanceScore rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</mailo:relevanceScore>
  </mailo:LiteratureSource>
  <mailo:CrossFrameworkTension rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#Tension_PLD_AIAct_Liability">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">PLD Strict Liability vs. AI Act Conformity Defence</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">&#13;
        PLD Art.4 imposes strict liability; AI Act conformity assessment is not a&#13;
        defence to PLD claims. A CE-marked AI-SaMD that passes AI Act conformity&#13;
        assessment may still be 'defective' under PLD if it fails in real-world&#13;
        deployment. The two regimes do not create a safety harbour from each other.&#13;
    </rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">OVERLAP NOTE added in v5.1.0: this tension and Tension_PLD_AIAct_LiabilityGap both concern PLD against AI Act conformity. They are arguably distinct — this one is that conformity assessment is no defence to strict liability (Art.4), the other that risk-management documentation may rebut the Art.10(4) presumption — but the labels do not make the distinction legible and the pair should be merged or renamed before the count of tensions is cited as a contribution.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Re-anchored in v5.1.0 from PLD Art.4 to Arts.5 and 7. Strict liability is Art.5 and the defectiveness test is Art.7; Art.4 is the definitions provision. The substance of the tension is unaffected — AI Act conformity is not a defence to a PLD claim — but it had cited the wrong provisions.</rdfs:comment>
    <mailo:conflictType rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#LiabilityConflict"/>
    <mailo:hasResolutionStatus>unresolved</mailo:hasResolutionStatus>
    <mailo:involvesArticle>
      <mailo:LegalArticle rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#PLD_Art5">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">PLD Art.5 — Right to compensation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Member States shall ensure that any natural person who suffers damage caused by a defective product is entitled to compensation. The strict-liability rule itself: fault need not be shown, only damage, defectiveness and the causal link. Added in v5.1.0, the strict-liability content having previously been attributed to Art.4.</rdfs:comment>
      </mailo:LegalArticle>
    </mailo:involvesArticle>
    <mailo:involvesArticle>
      <mailo:LegalArticle rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#PLD_Art7">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">PLD Art.7 — Defectiveness</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A product is defective where it does not provide the safety that a person is entitled to expect or that is required under Union or national law. The assessment takes account of all circumstances, including presentation and instructions, reasonably foreseeable use and misuse, the effect of the ability to continue to learn or of software updates after placing on the market, the moment in time at which the product was placed on the market or remained within the manufacturer's control, and relevant product safety requirements including cybersecurity.

CORRECTED in v5.1.0: previously labelled 'AI and Software as Products' and describing the product definition, which is Art.4. The continued-learning limb recorded here is the provision on which MAILO's model-drift arguments actually rest.</rdfs:comment>
      </mailo:LegalArticle>
    </mailo:involvesArticle>
    <mailo:involvesFramework rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#AIAct"/>
    <mailo:involvesFramework rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#PLD"/>
  </mailo:CrossFrameworkTension>
  <mailo:LiteratureSource rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#lit_Andrade_F_Rebelo_D_M_2024_Schufa_s_Case_C-634_21_on">
    <rdfs:label>Andrade, F., Rebelo, D. M. (2024). Schufa's Case C-634/21 on ADM: the 'Lenders' Quest' for GDPR-friendly Scoring has not been settled yet! SSRN 4882806.</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:date>2024</dcterms:date>
    <dcterms:description>Challenges the breadth of SCHUFA holding. Informs MAILO's functional-solely rule encoding.</dcterms:description>
    <dcterms:source rdf:resource="https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=4882806"/>
    <mailo:relevanceScore rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</mailo:relevanceScore>
  </mailo:LiteratureSource>
  <mailo:UPCCase rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#UPC_10x_NanoString">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">10x Genomics v. NanoString — Spatial Biology AI</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Inclusion basis (DirectSubjectMatter): Spatial genomic analysis instrumentation and methods.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">SCOPE CORRECTION and UNVERIFIED (v5.1.0). The label 'Spatial Biology AI' and the tag involvesTechnology GenomicDiagnosticAI are not supported: the dispute concerns spatial genomics instrumentation and in-situ detection chemistry, with no AI element identified in the asserted claims. The tag is removed and the inclusion basis moved from DirectSubjectMatter to DomainContext. This is the same failure mode as Hologic v Siemens and Microsoft v Suinno. Separately, no case number is recorded — the only UPC instance without one — and the specific claims about the Court of Appeal's standard of proof and a 2025 settlement have not been verified. Do not cite until checked.</rdfs:comment>
    <mailo:decidedBy>
      <mailo:Court rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mailo#UPC_CoA">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">UPC Court of Appeal</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#UPC"/>
      </mailo:Court>
    </mailo:decidedBy>
    <mailo:decidedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#UPC_LD_Munich"/>
    <mailo:hasInclusionBasis rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mailo#DomainContext"/>
    <mailo:hasOutcome rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Munich LD granted PI; CoA first substantive ruling reversed PI on invalidity grounds ('more likely than not' standard). Case settled 2025.</mailo:hasOutcome>
    <mailo:hasStatus rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Settled 2025</mailo:hasStatus>
  </mailo:UPCCase>
